---
import Icon from './Icon.astro';
import PlacesMap from './PlacesMap.astro';
const rows = [
  { icon: 'map', t: 'Region and territory', x: 'A coastal town west of Lisbon, between the Sintra mountains and the Atlantic Ocean. It is part of the area often called the Estoril Coast or Portuguese Riviera, a popular name rather than an official region. The Cascais council area (the município, roughly like a US county) covers about 97 km² (38 square miles) and had about 214,000 residents in the 2021 census. It has four parishes (freguesias, roughly like districts or neighborhoods): Cascais and Estoril, Alcabideche, Carcavelos and Parede, and São Domingos de Rana. Lisbon is about 18 miles (29 km) east.' },
  { icon: 'landmark', t: 'Early history', x: 'People lived in this area since prehistoric times, and the Romans left villas and fish-salting tanks. By the 12th century Cascais was a small village of fishermen and farmers under Sintra. King Pedro I made it a town (vila) on 7 June 1364. The 1755 earthquake almost destroyed it. In 1870 King Luís chose it as his seaside holiday home, and the court followed, which began its rise as a resort. [VERIFIED: Cascais council history page] The royal family spent summers here until 1908. [PROBABLY TRUE: Wikipedia only]' },
  { icon: 'landmark', t: 'Estoril history', x: 'Estoril was developed as a resort in the early 1900s, largely by Fausto de Figueiredo, who helped bring in the railway; the Hotel Palácio opened in 1930 and Casino Estoril in 1931. [PROBABLY TRUE: hotel history page and Wikipedia, dates differ in detail] Portugal stayed neutral in World War II, so Estoril became a haven for exiled royals and a meeting place for spies from both sides. [PROBABLY TRUE] Ian Fleming, then a British naval intelligence officer, is said to have stayed at the Palácio and met the double agent Duško Popov, often named as an inspiration for James Bond. [PROBABLY TRUE: hotel history page says Fleming stayed; Popov link is only "widely believed"] The claim that Casino Estoril inspired the novel Casino Royale is popular but rests on a travel article. [REASONABLY TRUE-UNVERIFIED]' },
  { icon: 'landmark', t: 'Culture', x: 'A cluster of nine museums within walking distance of each other, called the Museum Quarter, including the Casa das Histórias Paula Rego. The town museum is free. There are theatres, a Wednesday market, summer concerts and a street-art festival.' },
  { icon: 'trees', t: 'Nature', x: 'The coast sits next to the Sintra-Cascais Natural Park. Guides list 17 beaches. Guincho is windy and used for surfing, windsurfing and kitesurfing. Cabo da Roca, the westernmost point of mainland Europe, is about a half-hour drive away. A cycle and walking path runs from Cascais marina to Guincho.' },
  { icon: 'utensils', t: 'Cuisine', x: 'Food is a main reason people visit, and there are plenty of choices. Rua Amarela (Yellow Street) is a car-free street with about 40 restaurants, the Mercado da Vila has stalls and eateries, and the guides name good places all over town, from seafood and traditional Portuguese to Italian, Japanese and more. See Where to eat below.' },
  { icon: 'music', t: 'Nightlife', x: 'Bars cluster around Rua Frederico Arouca (a street) and Largo Luís de Camões (a square), with expat pubs and cocktail bars in the marina. Casino Estoril is described as one of the largest casinos in Europe. One guide calls the scene sociable rather than rowdy.' },
  { icon: 'train-front', t: 'Transportation', x: 'The Cascais railway line runs from Cais do Sodré station in Lisbon to Cascais, about 33 to 40 minutes. One guide says trains run every 10 to 12 minutes and that some change at Oeiras, while another calls it direct. The A5 motorway and a coastal road also lead to Lisbon, and Lisbon airport is the nearest.' },
  { icon: 'cloud-sun', t: 'Climate', x: 'A warm-summer Mediterranean climate: mild, wet winters and warm, dry summers. Hills and the western coast can be windy, foggy and cool.' },
];
---
<section id="cascais-about" class="about" aria-label="About Cascais">
  <h2>Cascais at a glance</h2>
  <p class="muted">A neutral overview drawn from tourism guides and Wikipedia, not fully confirmed against official sources; history rows carry their own labels. Handy Portuguese words: <em>praia</em> = beach, <em>rua</em> = street, <em>largo</em> = square, <em>mercado</em> = market, <em>câmara municipal</em> = city hall, <em>loja</em> = shop or service office.</p>
  <div class="ab-grid">
    {rows.map(r => (
      <div class="ab-row">
        <div class="ab-lab"><span class="ic"><Icon name={r.icon} /></span><h3>{r.t}</h3></div>
        <p>{r.x}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <PlacesMap />
  <p class="totop"><a href="#top">↑ Back to top</a></p>
</section>
<style is:global>
  .about { margin:1rem 0 1.2rem; background:var(--teal-soft); border-radius:20px; padding:.9rem 1.1rem 1rem; }
  .about h2 { font-size:1.15rem; margin:0 0 .2rem; color:var(--teal); }
  .about > p { margin:0 0 .8rem; font-size:.92rem; }
  .ab-grid { display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:.5rem; }
  .ab-row { display:grid; grid-template-columns:12.5rem minmax(0,1fr); gap:.4rem 1rem; align-items:start; background:var(--surface); border-radius:14px; padding:.6rem .9rem; }
  .ab-lab { display:flex; align-items:center; gap:.6rem; }
  .ab-row .ic { display:grid; place-items:center; flex:none; width:2rem; height:2rem; border-radius:50%; background:var(--teal-soft); color:var(--teal); } .ab-row .ic .i { --s:1.1rem; }
  .ab-row h3 { margin:0; font-size:.98rem; color:var(--teal); }
  .ab-row p { margin:0; font-size:.92rem; line-height:1.5; }
  @media (max-width:640px){ .ab-row { grid-template-columns:1fr; } }
  .ab-src { margin-top:.7rem; font-size:.88rem; } .ab-src summary { cursor:pointer; font-weight:700; color:var(--teal); }
  .ab-src ul { margin:.5rem 0 0; padding-left:1.2rem; } .ab-src li { margin:.25rem 0; }
  .phase-hero .also { font-size:.82rem; opacity:.9; margin:0 0 .3rem; }
</style>
